    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Experimental infrastructure exists (all models, benchmarks verified), but the core claim of per-instance binary accuracy prediction is seriously undermined: foundational papers (Date Fragments: Pearson r=-0.61 at aggregate group level; Token Tax: R²=0.20-0.50 at language level) show only aggregate correlations, and CharBench explicitly rejects tokenization as the primary failure cause for character-level tasks (NT correlation ≈ 0.000 for counting). Per-instance AUC may be only marginally above chance. Additionally, Qwen 3.5 235B does not exist — the largest is 397B-A17B."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The proposal is exceptionally well-articulated: clear problem definition, specific sub-metric formulations, concrete experimental plan with named models/benchmarks, and well-defined evaluation protocol (leave-one-task-out CV, Spearman ρ, AUC)."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "If per-instance prediction works, the diagnostic toolkit would be practically useful. But if prediction strength is weak (likely given the evidence), the contribution reduces to confirming aggregate trends already shown in separate papers. The 'task-agnostic' claim is overstated with only 4 task categories and 4 CV folds."
    }
